;;; A relation parameter its activator binds to `eq` heads a premise of one
;;; argument, and the run stops where the plan is compiled — M1f **W60**,
;;; register row `W60/eq-arity-premise` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), filed
;;; by S1f.5.9 (T1f.5.9.1, 2026-11-09), `open` and owned by S1f.5.9. The
;;; shape is the one S1f.5.18 found and left unfiled, `program_read.rs`' error
;;; past the load.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule `r` whose parameter `?R` heads the premise `(?R ?x)`, and its
;;; activator `(r eq)`, which binds `?R` to `eq`. `eq` is the relation of
;;; equality, between exactly two values (M1f S1f.5.7, Q-M1e2.26), so the
;;; premise is `(eq ?x)`, which the grammar refuses wherever it is written.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(eq ?x)` written in the `:match` is a parse error. Written through the
;;; parameter, the activator the program states binds it, so the loader sees
;;; it: the program is refused at load, one line naming `r`, `?R`, the form
;;; and the activator, and every command that loads it exits 1 (`ein test`:
;;; 2). A run that stops is no answer (Q-M1f.36's reading).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loads, and every command stops at root where `(r eq)`'s plan
;;; is compiled, exit 1, with the compiler's line in the Python IR's
;;; spelling, *`(eq …)` takes exactly 2 arguments and was given 1:
;;; SForm(head=Var(name='R'), …) at Loc(…)*, M1e S1e.2.1's arity check
;;; (`ein solve FILE` at `ee3da657`, release and debug alike).
;;;
;;; ── Since M1f S1f.5.9 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The loader refuses the program (T1f.5.9.4, 2026-11-09): every command
;;; that loads it exits 1 with one line, naming `r`, `?R`, the form and `(r eq)` (`ein test`: 2), in either
;;; build, where every command stopped where the plan was compiled. The register's `now` is that refusal; the section
;;; above is the record of the answer it replaced.

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a p")
(relation hit Node :why "{?1} is hit")
(p a)
(rule r (?R)
  :match (?R ?x)
  :assert (hit ?x)
  :why "{?x} stands in {?R}, so it is hit")
(r eq)
(query :goal (hit ?x))
